WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.835
- It's crazy.

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:03.103
Once again, if I
was to write this,

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:05.438
let's say 10 years ago,
as a political thriller,

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:06.940 align:start size:81% position:19%
it would go in the
science fiction

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.576
or the dystopian thriller
aisle at the bookstore.

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:11.144
No one would have believed it

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:12.612 align:start size:84% position:16%
if I had written the
exact same things

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.214
that are going on in
this country today,

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:15.181
and internationally,

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.683
and put that into a novel,

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:17.917
it would've been unbelievable.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:19.185
The reviews on Amazon would say,

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:21.421
"Ah, can't believe any of this.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:24.391
"This would never happen,"
that sort of thing, one star,

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:26.626
but lo and behold,
look what's going on.

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:29.796
(authoritative music)

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.001 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:38.138 line:0%
and joining me today
is a Navy SEAL sniper,

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:41.508 line:0%
and number-one "New York
Times" bestselling author

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:45.145 align:start size:97% position:3%
and now author of the
brand-new book "In the Blood,"

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:46.446
Jack Carr.

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:47.814
Finally, welcome to
"The Rubin Report."

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:49.215 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, thank you so
much for having me on.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.884
Great to see you.

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.286 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I have wanted to have
you on for quite some time.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:56.022
First off, I gotta
say, I had to say,

00:00:56.022 --> 00:01:00.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
number-one "New York
Times" bestselling author,

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:01.528
even though the "New York Times"

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.998 align:start size:97% position:3%
left me off their bestseller
list just weeks ago,

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:06.399
but you got the number-one spot.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:07.901
It's still credit to you,

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.803 align:start size:84% position:16%
so it sort of stabbed
me a little bit.

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:11.438
I'm kinda in the
blood right now,

00:01:11.438 --> 00:01:12.672
but you deserve the credit,

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.307
and I went ahead
and did it anyway.

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:15.775
- Well, I sincerely
appreciate that,

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.444
and as we know, it's hard.

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:18.878
The number one and number two,

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.147
maybe number-three
slots on that list,

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:22.615 line:0%
it's hard for them to ignore,

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.250 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
but they have some
flexibility down there

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:29.022 align:start position:0% line:0%
with those other ones as an
editorial, I think it was found,

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:30.690
I don't know, 30,
40, 50 years ago,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.392 align:start size:94% position:6%
whenever that was
brought up to the courts,

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:34.327
so they have a little
flexibility there,

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:36.296 align:start size:97% position:3%
but that's why the goal
has to always be number one

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:37.564
so they can't ignore you.

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:39.032
- Exactly. Exactly.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:40.834
Well, you did get number
one. It says it right here.

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.068
I just said to you,
before we started,

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:43.369 align:start size:84% position:16%
the cover of this
book is just awesome.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.039 align:start size:84% position:16%
The colors, it
just freaking hits.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.340
You got a couple
of them behind you

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.442 align:start size:84% position:16%
so first off, I just
gotta start with,

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.710
I mean, your whole
operation there.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.412
I'm feeling very
stuffy right now.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:54.214
You are obviously,

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:57.550 align:start size:84% position:16%
you are in your
bunker somewhere safe.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.185
What's going on over there?

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.019
- This is true.

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:01.554
Undisclosed location,

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:04.324
but I like to be surrounded by
things that mean a lot to me,

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:06.693 align:start size:97% position:3%
the crossed Winkler tomahawks
that are used in the book

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:09.596 align:start position:0%
and that Amazon actually
allowed us to use in the series

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:11.397 align:start size:88% position:13%
and feature prominently
with Chris Pratt

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.732
that's coming out this summer,

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:13.967
but probably like this one.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.402 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is Hemingway's
typewriter right here,

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:17.570 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Wow.
- and he wrote

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.605 align:start size:84% position:16%
You wrote "A Moveable
Feast" on this.

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.039
It went up for auction,

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:22.876 align:start size:97% position:3%
which a bunch of other
Hemingway stuff in January,

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:24.210
early February of 2020,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.013 align:start size:88% position:13%
so right before the
COVID panic really hit,

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.683
and a fan got it for me, and
didn't wanna be recognized,

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:34.254
but knew I had this,
just a fan of Hemingway

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:36.589
and thought that
I would enjoy this

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.358
and that I would appreciate it,

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:39.792
and so they sent it to me

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
with all the documentation
and the whole thing,

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:44.998 align:start size:97% position:3%
and yeah, it's crazy, so
that inspires me as I write.

00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:47.000 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Man, you've done
something right in life

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.434
when someone you don't know

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:51.070 align:start size:88% position:13%
buys you Ernest
Hemingway's typewriter.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:51.938
- It's crazy.

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:53.373 align:start size:75% position:25%
Crazy.
- Pretty solid.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:56.643 align:start position:0%
Pretty solid, so for my
audience that does not know you

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.745 align:start size:84% position:16%
or only knows about
you from your writing,

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.013
et cetera, et cetera,

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:01.581 align:start size:81% position:19%
gimme a little bit
of the history,

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.082
the Navy SEAL, all that stuff

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:06.252 align:start position:0%
'cause you you have pretty
extraordinary history yourself.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:07.187
- Well, I appreciate that,

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:09.122
and yeah, I feel very fortunate

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:10.657
in that I always knew

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:12.759
I wanted to serve my country
in uniform, very early age.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:14.260 align:start size:84% position:16%
My grandfather served
in World War II

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.828
and didn't make it home.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:18.898
He was killed in the
Pacific in May of 1945,

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:20.500
but I grew up with the maps

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:21.935 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they used to give
aviators back then,

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:23.536
which were silk so
that if they got wet,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:24.671
you could still use them,

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:26.339 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they didn't
disintegrate like paper,

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.575 align:start position:0%
pictures of him and his
squadron, his medals, his wings.

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:30.143
He was a Marine aviator,

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.946 align:start position:0%
so I was just drawn to the
military from a very early age,

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:35.181 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then, at the ripe
old age of seven,

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:38.318
I found out about SEALs,
and my mom was a librarian,

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:40.687 align:start size:97% position:3%
so went down to the local
library to do some research,

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:42.722 align:start size:88% position:13%
and there wasn't very
much written about SEALs

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.057
or special operations back then.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.025 align:start size:88% position:13%
There were a few
articles here and there,

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:47.360
couple chapters
in a book or two,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
but my takeaway that,
"Hey, these guys

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.398 align:start position:0%
"are some of the most elite
special operators in the world,

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:53.933
"and the training is
some of the toughest

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:56.302
"ever devised by a
modern military,"

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:57.937
so whether that was true or not,

00:03:57.937 --> 00:03:59.239
seven-year-old Jack was all in,

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:02.175
and the recruiter had to
do nothing, essentially.

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.676
I made their job very easy,

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
so I kept my eyes on that
for, really, my entire life,

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:08.281
but I also started
reading the books

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
that my parents were reading
in about fifth grade,

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:11.517
so that's when Tom Clancy's

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:12.986 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Hunt for Red
October" comes out,

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:14.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I'm reading David
Morrell and Nelson DeMille

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.990 align:start size:94% position:6%
and A.J. Quinnell and J.C.
Pollock and Marc Olden

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.925
and all these guys
whose protagonists,

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.761
whose main characters had
backgrounds in Vietnam,

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.863
either as like Marine
snipers back then,

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:27.033
or it was Navy SEALs
or Army Special Forces

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.268
or CIA paramilitary.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:29.602
That was the typical background

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:32.105
of your '80s action hero
in the thriller genre,

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:33.740
and then on screen as well,

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.209
so it was so magical for
me to read these books

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:38.778
and those guys were
really my professors

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:40.313
in the art of storytelling,

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.215
although I didn't look at
it like that at the time.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.449
I was just a fan,

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I knew that after
my time in the military,

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:47.020
I would write books like this,

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:49.255 align:start size:97% position:3%
the books that I was
enjoying as a kid growing up,

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:50.657
so I never stopped that reading,

00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:52.358
never stopped that
studying of warfare,

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.227 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether the terrorism
insurgencies,

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:56.562
counterinsurgencies,
special operations,

00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:59.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
that was all just a
part of my upbringing,

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:00.333
and it was part
of the foundation

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:02.268 align:start size:88% position:13%
that allowed me to be
the operator that I was,

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:03.803
the leader that I was
in the SEAL teams,

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:06.406
and then, all of that comes
together as I'm getting out

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:07.674
to write these thrillers,

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:09.676
so I did 20 years
in the SEAL team,

00:05:09.676 --> 00:05:11.277 align:start size:84% position:16%
started out enlisted,
was a sniper,

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:13.780 align:start position:0%
then became an officer, finished
up as a troop commander,

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.748
and interestingly enough,

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.984
that's where the reader
meets my protagonist

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:19.152
in my first novel.

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:20.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
He's a troop commander
on his way out,

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.889
and then disaster and
conspiracy strikes,

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.891
and off we go to the races.

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:27.160 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Crazy how those
things connect, huh?

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.695 align:start size:81% position:19%
- It is, and it
wasn't the intent.

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:30.530
I thought there were gonna be
two separate chapters in life,

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.498
but when I sat down to
write that first one,

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.500
it became readily apparent
that it was gonna be more

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.704 align:start position:0%
than just the technical details
of a sniper weapon system

00:05:37.704 --> 00:05:39.105
or something like that.

00:05:39.105 --> 00:05:41.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was really the
emotions and the feelings

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.009
behind certain events
that I was involved in

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:45.411 align:start position:0%
that I could bring to a
completely fictional narrative,

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:47.347 align:start size:88% position:13%
so if my main
character, James Reece,

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:49.415 align:start size:84% position:16%
is getting ambushed
in Los Angeles,

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:50.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
I can go back and
remember what it was like

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:53.820
to be ambushed in Baghdad in
2006 at the height of the war,

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.421
and I can take those
feelings and emotions

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.790 align:start position:0%
and apply them to this
completely fictional narrative,

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is why I think it stood
out to Simon &amp; Schuster,

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:02.295
who sees thousands
of books like these

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:04.163
cross their desks every year.

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:08.468 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, gimme the
best and worst moment

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:10.002
of being a SEAL.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:13.539
I suspect I can guess the
worst, but maybe the best,

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:14.774
- Yeah, the worst, obviously,

00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:16.542
is when your friend
doesn't make it home,

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.077
and you go to those funerals,

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:19.746 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you pound your
trident into the coffin,

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:22.515
and you see that
widow or the kids,

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:26.552
and so that's obviously
the worst part of it,

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.020
but the best part of it,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.722 align:start size:97% position:3%
gosh, I never really thought
of the best part of it,

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:31.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I think it's the
entire experience

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:33.259
in that you have a purpose.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:34.527
You have a mission.

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:36.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
Your passion is being
the best operator

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:37.730
that you can possibly be.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.699
You owe that to the guys
to your right and left,

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:43.603 align:start position:0%
to their families, to the team,
to the country as a whole,

00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:45.037
to that entire mission,

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.506
so I think it's that.

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.975 align:start size:88% position:13%
I guess it would be
the having that purpose

00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:50.343
that is so clearly defined,

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:51.611
and when you're down range

00:06:51.611 --> 00:06:53.613
being solely focused
on that task at hand

00:06:53.613 --> 00:06:55.148
and not having to worry
about anything else,

00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:57.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
not worrying about the
leaky faucet or the gutters

00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:00.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
or the baby that's
crying or whatever it is,

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:01.888
you were solely focused
on that task at hand,

00:07:01.888 --> 00:07:04.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
building patterns of life
on your targeted individuals

00:07:04.457 --> 00:07:05.658
and then going out

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:06.526
and grabbing them in
the middle of the night

00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:07.460
to bring them back,

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:08.694
ask them a couple questions,

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:11.063 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then go take out the
rest of that network,

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:14.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
so having that purpose
that is so clear, I think,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.636 align:start size:81% position:19%
is probably one
of the best parts.

00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:18.805
- So one of the things
that I'm always amazed by

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:22.475 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that those of you guys that
have been in the military,

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:25.611
been SEALs, been in the
Navy, whatever it is,

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.980 align:start size:88% position:13%
or people that have been
in the CIA, the FBI.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.182
Just a couple weeks ago,

00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:30.716
I had Bryan Dean
Wright on the show.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:33.019
I don't know if who he is,
but he's a former CIA guy.

00:07:34.187 --> 00:07:36.189
People that get out
and then do things

00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:38.090 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are ancillarily
related to this,

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.993
whether it's talking on
YouTube for a living,

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:42.528
whether it's writing books

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:44.997
that are either fiction or
nonfiction, everything else,

00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:46.666
how do you blend?

00:07:46.666 --> 00:07:47.934
You know some stuff

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:49.602
that they probably don't
want you to talk about.

00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.037
There's a way

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:52.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you have to fictionize
some of these things.

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:54.373
You don't wanna get in trouble.

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:56.209 align:start position:0%
You don't wanna become a
character in one of your books.

00:07:56.209 --> 00:07:58.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
How do you take
all of those things

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:00.580
and make it honest and sensible

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:04.350
and also not so you're gonna
get yourself killed, literally.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.618
- (chuckles) Right, so
a lot of people think,

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:06.986
on the outside looking in,

00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:09.255
that Navy SEALs know all
sorts of secret stuff,

00:08:09.255 --> 00:08:11.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
and maybe there's a couple
things here and there,

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:13.192
but really, what we're doing is

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:15.828
what every major city SWAT
team is gonna do tonight.

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:16.963
They're gonna go
serve a warrant.

00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:18.431
They're gonna go do

00:08:18.431 --> 00:08:20.266
either a no-knock or a
knock warrant somewhere.

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:22.835 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're gonna bust in, and
they're gonna grab a fugitive,

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:24.103
so we're doing that same thing

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:26.439
except ours happen to
be in Baghdad or Kabul

00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:28.007 align:start size:84% position:16%
or somewhere
somewhere like that,

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:30.443 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we have a few more
maybe assets at our disposal

00:08:30.443 --> 00:08:33.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
to build these targets,
build these target packages

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:35.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then go prosecute
these targets,

00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:36.883 align:start size:84% position:16%
but there's not much
secret about it.

00:08:36.883 --> 00:08:38.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, you're just,
you're going up

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:39.585
and kicking a door in
the middle of the night

00:08:39.585 --> 00:08:41.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
and grabbing somebody outta
their bed, essentially,

00:08:42.788 --> 00:08:45.224
but I still wanted to make sure

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:47.627
that I was complying
with all the everything

00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:48.861
that I had signed
in the military,

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:50.863
and I didn't wanna
give anything away,

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:53.132
and I was still closely related,

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:55.768 align:start position:0%
closely connected to my military
time for that first novel,

00:08:55.768 --> 00:08:57.470
so I submitted it to the...

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:58.938
I got some lawyers,

00:08:58.938 --> 00:09:00.206
and they said, "Yeah, we
recommend you submit this,"

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:01.607
so I did,

00:09:01.607 --> 00:09:02.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they took out, I
think, like nine sentences,

00:09:02.675 --> 00:09:03.509
so not much.

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:04.744
It was pretty good,

00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:06.712 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they got back
to me with, 45 days.

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:08.247
They advertise 30,

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:10.750
and what you sign says they
will get back to you in 30.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.251
They got back to me in 45.

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:14.120
I thought that was pretty
good for the government,

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.022
so I did that for the
second one as well.

00:09:16.022 --> 00:09:17.623
That one, one month went by,

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:20.126
then two, then three,
then four, then seven,

00:09:20.126 --> 00:09:22.295
and we had to push
the publication date,

00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:23.696
and they got back,

00:09:23.696 --> 00:09:26.966 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they took out 54
sentences, passages, words,

00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:28.401
so at this point,

00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:31.237 align:start position:0%
I can afford a little bit,
afford some more attorney fees,

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:32.838
so we go, and we appeal,

00:09:32.838 --> 00:09:35.374
and we tie each and every
one of those redactions

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:37.310
to a publicly available
government document,

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:39.545 align:start size:88% position:13%
so not to someone else's
book or to Wikipedia,

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:41.814 line:0%
but something anyone in
the world can download

00:09:41.814 --> 00:09:43.583 line:0%
from our very own government.

00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:45.184 line:0%
They let me win on 37,

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:46.919 line:0%
so now you can go
to the paperback,

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:48.187
and you can look in there,

00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:49.655
and you compare it
to the hardcover,

00:09:49.655 --> 00:09:51.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you can see what they
were concerned about,

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:53.225
but that I won on appeal.

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:54.827
One of those things,

00:09:54.827 --> 00:09:58.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
I made up a completely
fictitious CIA black
site in Morocco.

00:09:58.364 --> 00:09:59.498
They took out Morocco.

00:09:59.498 --> 00:10:00.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
They took out
Moorish architecture.

00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:02.468
They took out the
Atlas Mountains.

00:10:02.468 --> 00:10:05.871 align:start size:97% position:3%
They took out everything
that could pinpoint where it,

00:10:05.871 --> 00:10:07.139
what country this,

00:10:07.139 --> 00:10:08.774
and I had no, in my
military experience,

00:10:08.774 --> 00:10:11.377 align:start size:97% position:3%
no touchpoints with Moroccan
intelligence services

00:10:11.377 --> 00:10:12.645
or military, anything like that.

00:10:12.645 --> 00:10:14.814
I just made it up, but
by doing that appeal,

00:10:14.814 --> 00:10:15.748
what are they telling us?

00:10:15.748 --> 00:10:16.949
Well, now they're telling us

00:10:16.949 --> 00:10:17.883 align:start size:84% position:16%
that there's probably
a CIA black site-

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:18.851
(Dave laughing drowns out Jack)

00:10:18.851 --> 00:10:20.052
so if they'd done nothing,

00:10:20.052 --> 00:10:21.454
it would've been more
beneficial to them

00:10:21.454 --> 00:10:22.922
than had they gone
through this process,

00:10:22.922 --> 00:10:24.924
so I did that for the
first three novels.

00:10:24.924 --> 00:10:27.660 align:start position:0%
The third one, when we appealed,
they got back and said,

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:29.695 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Hey, we're not gonna
let you appeal,"

00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:32.398 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I took that as,
"Quit bothering us, kid,

00:10:32.398 --> 00:10:33.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
"with this fiction."
(Dave laughs)

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:35.134
- We're not gonna
let you appeal.

00:10:35.134 --> 00:10:36.602 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's probably the
best thing you can hear

00:10:36.602 --> 00:10:37.770
from the government

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:38.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
in your shoes.
- Yeah, I'll take it.

00:10:38.804 --> 00:10:40.539
I'll take it, so
the next one was

00:10:40.539 --> 00:10:42.508 align:start size:97% position:3%
I went deep down the rabbit
hole, in my fourth one,

00:10:42.508 --> 00:10:45.011
into bioweapons and
bioweapons research,

00:10:45.011 --> 00:10:46.979 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so I'm glad I
didn't submit that one.

00:10:46.979 --> 00:10:48.681
It was all, once again,

00:10:48.681 --> 00:10:50.850
I had no touchpoints with
bioweapons in the military,

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.184 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I went deep down
the rabbit hole,

00:10:52.184 --> 00:10:53.919 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then, on this one,
on "In the Blood,"

00:10:53.919 --> 00:10:55.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
deep down the rabbit hole
on quantum computing,

00:10:55.788 --> 00:10:59.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
artificial intelligence
and mass data surveillance

00:10:59.125 --> 00:11:01.193
and data storage
of U.S. citizens,

00:11:01.193 --> 00:11:04.563
and that was more terrifying
than the bioweapons research

00:11:04.563 --> 00:11:06.198
that I did on the
previous novel.

00:11:07.233 --> 00:11:08.501
- That sorta tells you

00:11:08.501 --> 00:11:11.037
everything you need
to know about that,

00:11:12.638 --> 00:11:14.540
so you're making the movie,

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:16.042
and as you mentioned,

00:11:16.042 --> 00:11:17.810 align:start size:84% position:16%
Chris Pratt is gonna
be in the movie,

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:20.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Chris Pratt, who's
an awesome actor,

00:11:20.479 --> 00:11:23.149
and what I think is so
interesting about him is

00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:25.818
the guy has managed
to stay in Hollywood

00:11:25.818 --> 00:11:30.823
despite his politics
being outside of the
norm of Hollywood.

00:11:32.024 --> 00:11:33.693
How involved were
you in the casting

00:11:33.693 --> 00:11:35.428 align:start size:84% position:16%
and just the creation
of the movie?

00:11:35.428 --> 00:11:38.431
Because, obviously,
you writing a book,

00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:41.167
doing it in your little
operation over there,

00:11:41.167 --> 00:11:42.735
or I don't know how,
I can't say little,

00:11:42.735 --> 00:11:45.271
but your very cool operation
over there is very different

00:11:45.271 --> 00:11:47.106
than when you hand your
baby over to Hollywood,

00:11:47.106 --> 00:11:49.141
and Hollywood may
not be so in line

00:11:49.141 --> 00:11:50.676
with perhaps some of
the things in the book,

00:11:50.676 --> 00:11:52.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
perhaps the type of people
that you want involved,

00:11:52.044 --> 00:11:53.345
et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:55.181
- Exactly, I didn't know
what to expect going in,

00:11:55.181 --> 00:11:56.582
just like I didn't
know what to expect

00:11:56.582 --> 00:11:57.850
from Simon &amp; Schuster going in.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:00.352
I thought they might
wanna change everything

00:12:00.352 --> 00:12:01.620
and give me some,

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:04.390
at least maybe hint that
maybe I shouldn't be...

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:05.391
Do you really have
to put this in there

00:12:05.391 --> 00:12:06.826
about the Second Amendment?

00:12:06.826 --> 00:12:08.894 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or does your character really
have to think this way?

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:11.363
They have never even hinted
at me changing anything.

00:12:11.363 --> 00:12:14.133
I've had complete creative
control, which is amazing,

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:15.334
but when you option something,

00:12:15.334 --> 00:12:17.403
especially when it's
before your first novel

00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:18.904
even hits shelves,

00:12:18.904 --> 00:12:21.006
you're not really negotiating
from a position of strength,

00:12:21.006 --> 00:12:24.110 align:start position:0%
and so they have all the rights,
can do whatever they want,

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:25.411
and so when you're
in that position,

00:12:25.411 --> 00:12:26.812
there's a lot of trust involved,

00:12:26.812 --> 00:12:29.115 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I was so lucky
that I had Chris Pratt,

00:12:29.115 --> 00:12:31.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
Antoine Fuqua as the director,
an incredible show runner,

00:12:31.817 --> 00:12:34.820
and the three of us just got
together in the beginning,

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:36.255 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what was
important to all of us

00:12:36.255 --> 00:12:38.824 align:start size:84% position:16%
was keeping that
dark, gritty, violent,

00:12:38.824 --> 00:12:40.926
primal nature of the book

00:12:40.926 --> 00:12:42.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause that's really what
resonated with people

00:12:42.728 --> 00:12:45.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
and broke it out from
maybe others in the genre,

00:12:45.598 --> 00:12:47.433 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the way that Chris Pratt
even found out about it

00:12:47.433 --> 00:12:48.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
before it hit shelves
is interesting

00:12:48.968 --> 00:12:52.338 align:start position:0%
in that I got a call from a
SEAL buddy in November of 2017,

00:12:52.338 --> 00:12:54.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
so a few months before
the book's gonna come out,

00:12:54.406 --> 00:12:56.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
and first he asked if I
remembered him, which I did,

00:12:56.675 --> 00:12:58.244
and then he asked

00:12:58.244 --> 00:12:59.145
if I remembered what I did
for him in the SEAL teams,

00:12:59.145 --> 00:13:00.346
which I did not,

00:13:00.346 --> 00:13:02.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
and he said, "You're
the only person

00:13:02.148 --> 00:13:03.816
"that sat me down
in your office,

00:13:03.816 --> 00:13:05.651
"helped me through
the transitioning
out of the military,

00:13:05.651 --> 00:13:07.052
"introduced me to people
in the private sector.

00:13:07.052 --> 00:13:08.287
"Nobody else did that.

00:13:08.287 --> 00:13:09.488
"Always wanted to
say thank you,"

00:13:09.488 --> 00:13:10.990
and I said, "How's it going?"

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:12.124
And he said, "Well,
it's going great,

00:13:12.124 --> 00:13:13.592
"but I heard you wrote a book,

00:13:13.592 --> 00:13:14.560 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I said, "Yeah, it's
coming out in a few months.

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:15.861
"I can send you a galley copy.

00:13:15.861 --> 00:13:17.296
"It's like a rough draft thing,"

00:13:17.296 --> 00:13:18.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
and he said, "Yeah,
I'd like that,

00:13:18.864 --> 00:13:20.199 align:start size:88% position:13%
"but I'd like to give
it to a friend of mine,"

00:13:20.199 --> 00:13:21.200
and I said, "Who's that?"

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:22.935
And he said, "Chris Pratt,"

00:13:22.935 --> 00:13:24.670
and what was even more
interesting about that

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.338
is that as I was writing,

00:13:26.338 --> 00:13:28.908
I thought of Chris Pratt
playing the main character,

00:13:28.908 --> 00:13:31.477 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I thought of
Antoine Fuqua directing,

00:13:31.477 --> 00:13:33.479
and another SEAL buddy
got it to Antoine Fuqua,

00:13:33.479 --> 00:13:34.914
who wanted it,

00:13:34.914 --> 00:13:36.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
and Chris and Antoine
wanted it at the same time,

00:13:36.448 --> 00:13:38.083
so finally, Antoine called Chris

00:13:38.083 --> 00:13:39.552
and said, "Let's
do this together,"

00:13:39.552 --> 00:13:41.554
and now we're all executive
producers on this thing,

00:13:41.554 --> 00:13:44.523
but it's a credit to Amazon

00:13:44.523 --> 00:13:46.625
for the things that they could,

00:13:46.625 --> 00:13:49.495 align:start position:0%
well, they were concerned about
a few things from the book,

00:13:49.495 --> 00:13:51.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
but really, all that ended
up being was concern,

00:13:51.463 --> 00:13:53.866 align:start size:84% position:16%
and once we talked
about different scenes

00:13:53.866 --> 00:13:56.535
that we needed in there and
wanted to have in there,

00:13:56.535 --> 00:13:58.103 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the fans would
want in there,

00:13:58.103 --> 00:14:00.306 align:start position:0%
they came down on the right
side of it each and every time,

00:14:00.306 --> 00:14:03.175
as uncomfortable as it
may have been for them,

00:14:03.175 --> 00:14:05.578 align:start size:84% position:16%
they sided with us,
and it's all in there,

00:14:05.578 --> 00:14:08.848
so couldn't be happier
with how that worked out.

00:14:08.848 --> 00:14:13.018
- What do you make of the
state of sort of the...

00:14:13.018 --> 00:14:15.788
Well, what do you call
the genre, actually?

00:14:15.788 --> 00:14:18.224 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like, military
thriller, or what are-

00:14:18.224 --> 00:14:19.625
- Thriller, if you have
to put it in something.

00:14:19.625 --> 00:14:21.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a political thriller,
for the most part,

00:14:21.093 --> 00:14:23.229
but there's all sorts
of thrillers out there.

00:14:23.229 --> 00:14:25.564
There's legal thrillers
and medical thrillers

00:14:25.564 --> 00:14:28.667 align:start size:88% position:13%
and dystopian thrillers
and all that stuff,

00:14:28.667 --> 00:14:29.935
but yeah, it's a
political thriller.

00:14:29.935 --> 00:14:31.170
- So political thrillers,

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:32.504
and I think of the
more in certain,

00:14:32.504 --> 00:14:33.739 align:start size:84% position:16%
like there's certain
political ones

00:14:33.739 --> 00:14:35.207
that are very much
in the politics part

00:14:35.207 --> 00:14:37.309 align:start position:0%
and then ones that are a little
more in the military part.

00:14:37.309 --> 00:14:38.777
I can think of so many.
You mentioned Tom Clancy.

00:14:38.777 --> 00:14:41.981
I mean, I can think of
so many incredible movies

00:14:41.981 --> 00:14:44.817 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the '80s or
really sorta early '90s

00:14:44.817 --> 00:14:46.652 align:start size:84% position:16%
that were just like,
there were just,

00:14:46.652 --> 00:14:48.787
every other week there was
just another awesome movie.

00:14:48.787 --> 00:14:51.457
You mentioned "Hunt
for Red October."

00:14:51.457 --> 00:14:54.426 align:start size:97% position:3%
What do you make of the
state of the genre right now?

00:14:54.426 --> 00:14:56.962
Because everything is so
politicized these days.

00:14:56.962 --> 00:15:01.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
I find, when I open up
Netflix or Hulu or Amazon,

00:15:03.068 --> 00:15:04.536
for me, personally,
as a politics guy,

00:15:04.536 --> 00:15:06.372
I'm often trying to avoid
politics a little bit.

00:15:06.372 --> 00:15:07.873
That's just me,

00:15:07.873 --> 00:15:09.375
but what do you make of the
general state of things?

00:15:09.375 --> 00:15:11.010
And I'm a guy that
I love explosions.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:13.178 align:start size:88% position:13%
I love thrillers. I love
all of these movies.

00:15:13.178 --> 00:15:15.047
I'm just curious what
your take on that is.

00:15:15.047 --> 00:15:17.283
- Yeah, it was something that
I was concerned about going in

00:15:17.283 --> 00:15:20.252 align:start size:97% position:3%
that other people that were
involved in the project

00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:21.587
were concerned about as well

00:15:21.587 --> 00:15:23.055
because, as we all know,

00:15:23.055 --> 00:15:24.990 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you've scrolled through
any of the streaming platforms

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.592
or gone to a movie recently,

00:15:26.592 --> 00:15:27.860
it seems like a
lot of the movies

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:30.496
are made for people
in L.A. or New York,

00:15:30.496 --> 00:15:32.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
and forget about
everyone in between,

00:15:32.865 --> 00:15:34.133
so we really wanted to make sure

00:15:34.133 --> 00:15:36.201
we didn't forget about
everybody in between,

00:15:36.201 --> 00:15:37.836
and it was interesting

00:15:37.836 --> 00:15:40.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
in that a lot of people's
view of Hollywood

00:15:40.906 --> 00:15:43.709
comes from seeing maybe a
clip of the Academy Awards

00:15:43.709 --> 00:15:45.377
or a Golden Globes
and (indistinct)

00:15:45.377 --> 00:15:47.413
an actor, a director,
or a producer on stage

00:15:47.413 --> 00:15:48.981
saying something kinda crazy,

00:15:48.981 --> 00:15:50.215
and you're kinda like,

00:15:50.215 --> 00:15:51.717
"Oh, Hollywood people
are all totally weird,"

00:15:51.717 --> 00:15:53.986 align:start size:97% position:3%
and, "Oh, my goodness, I can
see where this is going,"

00:15:53.986 --> 00:15:54.887
but on set,

00:15:56.021 --> 00:15:59.525 align:start size:88% position:13%
I had 350 people on
set making this series.

00:15:59.525 --> 00:16:02.094
It's an eight-part
series for Amazon,

00:16:02.094 --> 00:16:05.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
and (indistinct) this
thing called video village,

00:16:05.164 --> 00:16:06.665
so the executive producers,

00:16:06.665 --> 00:16:07.766
and we have our screens, and
we have our headphones on,

00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:09.001
and we can watch what's going on

00:16:09.001 --> 00:16:10.869
and make notes and
all the rest of it,

00:16:10.869 --> 00:16:12.338 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the other executive
producers told me

00:16:12.338 --> 00:16:13.672
they hadn't seen this before,

00:16:13.672 --> 00:16:16.875
but so many people on
that set would come up,

00:16:16.875 --> 00:16:18.377
track me down there,

00:16:18.377 --> 00:16:19.712
and like the hair and makeup
person would come in and say,

00:16:19.712 --> 00:16:22.247
"Hey, my son is going
to boot camp tomorrow.

00:16:22.247 --> 00:16:23.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
"He's a huge fan. He
follows you on Instagram.

00:16:23.949 --> 00:16:25.150
"Would you sign a book?"

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:26.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I always had a
big box of books there

00:16:26.352 --> 00:16:27.386
and would sign away,

00:16:27.386 --> 00:16:28.620
or someone would
come up and say,

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.589
"Hey, my son's going on
his first deployment.

00:16:30.589 --> 00:16:31.857
"Can you sign this for him?"

00:16:31.857 --> 00:16:34.159
And absolutely, and so
we'd talk about guns.

00:16:34.159 --> 00:16:37.730 align:start size:97% position:3%
We'd talk about Land Cruisers.
We'd talk about knives.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:40.733
We'd talk about hunting and
just normal people talk,

00:16:40.733 --> 00:16:43.002
and what was so extraordinary

00:16:43.002 --> 00:16:45.471
is that they're so
good at their job,

00:16:45.471 --> 00:16:48.040
but also, they just wanna put
food on their family's table,

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:51.343 align:start position:0%
and these are just normal
Americans that are working hard,

00:16:51.343 --> 00:16:52.878
and we had American flags up,

00:16:52.878 --> 00:16:55.147
all the Teamsters truck had
their American flags flying,

00:16:55.147 --> 00:16:57.149
and it was a special set,

00:16:57.149 --> 00:16:58.417
and people made a
point to tell me

00:16:58.417 --> 00:16:59.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they've been
on hundreds of sets,

00:16:59.885 --> 00:17:04.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they've never felt one
that was as open and welcoming

00:17:04.023 --> 00:17:05.657
and encouraging and positive

00:17:05.657 --> 00:17:08.127
and just a cool and
American as this one,

00:17:08.127 --> 00:17:11.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I thought that was, that
made me feel good anyway.

00:17:11.263 --> 00:17:14.133
- I can tell you, as somebody
that just fled Los Angeles

00:17:14.133 --> 00:17:15.367
after eight years there,

00:17:15.367 --> 00:17:16.969
there were awful lot
of people that I knew

00:17:16.969 --> 00:17:18.871
that were in the biz
that were waking up,

00:17:18.871 --> 00:17:20.672 align:start size:88% position:13%
that were sort of
lefties their whole life

00:17:20.672 --> 00:17:22.674
or were into a lot
of the craziness,

00:17:22.674 --> 00:17:24.209
and suddenly they were
going, "Wait a minute.

00:17:24.209 --> 00:17:25.644
"I can't get a job

00:17:25.644 --> 00:17:27.713 align:start size:84% position:16%
"'cause I'm a
60-something-year-old
white guy,"

00:17:27.713 --> 00:17:30.416 align:start position:0%
or, "I don't fit this diversity
thing," or something else,

00:17:30.416 --> 00:17:33.118
or the street that they
live on was burning down,

00:17:33.118 --> 00:17:35.054
and they suddenly got guns.

00:17:35.054 --> 00:17:36.522 align:start size:81% position:19%
I was one of those
people in L.A.

00:17:36.522 --> 00:17:38.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
that didn't have guns
before all this craziness,

00:17:38.891 --> 00:17:40.793 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then, suddenly, reality
hits you in the face,

00:17:40.793 --> 00:17:43.028
and you kinda change
your view on things.

00:17:43.028 --> 00:17:45.230
- Yeah, I got so many texts,

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:46.432
first, when COVID hit,

00:17:46.432 --> 00:17:47.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
and people were a
little worried about,

00:17:47.666 --> 00:17:48.901
"Hey, is there gonna
be food on the shelf,"

00:17:48.901 --> 00:17:50.335
or, "Is somebody gonna be there

00:17:50.335 --> 00:17:52.504 align:start size:84% position:16%
"at the other end
of when I call 911?"

00:17:52.504 --> 00:17:53.939
Then, of course,

00:17:53.939 --> 00:17:56.542 align:start position:0%
when the summer of civil unrest
hit us a few months later,

00:17:56.542 --> 00:17:59.645 align:start size:97% position:3%
the same thing: I was
getting texts and phone calls

00:17:59.645 --> 00:18:02.481
and e-mails from people in
California, in particular,

00:18:02.481 --> 00:18:03.849
asking about what
gun should I get?

00:18:03.849 --> 00:18:05.350
And then them being surprised

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:07.619
that they just can't run
down and just get one,

00:18:07.619 --> 00:18:10.989
just pay in cash and walk
out with one the same day,

00:18:10.989 --> 00:18:12.291 align:start size:84% position:16%
or why they couldn't
get ammunition

00:18:12.291 --> 00:18:14.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
sent to their house
in Los Angeles.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.262 align:start size:88% position:13%
These things were a
shock to a lot of people

00:18:17.262 --> 00:18:19.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
who just from what you
think in the news is that,

00:18:19.331 --> 00:18:20.799
"Oh yeah, anybody
can just walk in.

00:18:20.799 --> 00:18:23.702 align:start size:97% position:3%
"You walk out with some
fully automatic, boom-boom,

00:18:23.702 --> 00:18:25.237
"and stacks of ammo
and all that stuff,"

00:18:25.237 --> 00:18:28.006
yet they've been voting people
into office there for so long

00:18:28.006 --> 00:18:30.542
that they don't have
those rights anymore.

00:18:30.542 --> 00:18:33.679
They've outsourced those
to a government that is,

00:18:36.815 --> 00:18:39.351
whose leaders are surrounded
by taxpayer-funded security

00:18:39.351 --> 00:18:41.653
when they're trying to
take your rights away,

00:18:41.653 --> 00:18:43.722
and we see that, of course,
all across the country now,

00:18:43.722 --> 00:18:45.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
but California,
Chicago, of course,

00:18:45.858 --> 00:18:48.627
a couple other, New
York stands out,

00:18:48.627 --> 00:18:50.295
stand out from the
crowd in that regard,

00:18:50.295 --> 00:18:52.131
so I did get a lot of
phone calls like that,

00:18:52.131 --> 00:18:53.499 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there were a lot
of shocked people

00:18:53.499 --> 00:18:54.733
that they couldn't just go in

00:18:54.733 --> 00:18:56.368
and get any firearm
that they wanted.

00:18:56.368 --> 00:18:58.303
- You are preaching
my language, brother.

00:18:58.303 --> 00:19:00.739
Actually, I can't say
who it is out loud,

00:19:00.739 --> 00:19:02.541
but when all hell was
breaking loose in L.A.,

00:19:02.541 --> 00:19:04.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
I happened to say
on the show one day,

00:19:04.042 --> 00:19:06.245
"I'm thinking about getting
guns for the first time,"

00:19:06.245 --> 00:19:10.082 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I got a call from a
major Hollywood sitcom star,

00:19:10.082 --> 00:19:14.319
a woman who would never want
me to say her name on air,

00:19:14.319 --> 00:19:16.955
and she said, "Hey, if you
need guns, let me know,"

00:19:16.955 --> 00:19:19.124 align:start size:94% position:6%
and she ended up
connecting me with people,

00:19:19.124 --> 00:19:20.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's how I
was able to get guns

00:19:20.993 --> 00:19:22.194
'cause yeah, you're right:

00:19:22.194 --> 00:19:23.362
you couldn't get
ammo either in L.A.

00:19:23.362 --> 00:19:24.863
because it was,

00:19:24.863 --> 00:19:27.399 align:start size:97% position:3%
because so many people were
flipping on all of this stuff.

00:19:27.399 --> 00:19:28.333
Do you-
- Yeah-

00:19:28.333 --> 00:19:29.134
- Do you feel a certain...

00:19:29.134 --> 00:19:30.269
Oh, sorry. Go ahead.

00:19:30.269 --> 00:19:31.737 align:start size:88% position:13%
- No, I said you
couldn't even order it.

00:19:31.737 --> 00:19:35.207
You can't even order it and get
it delivered in Los Angeles.

00:19:35.207 --> 00:19:36.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that's just
a county thing,

00:19:36.675 --> 00:19:38.677 align:start size:97% position:3%
but anyway, yeah, they make
it extremely difficult.

00:19:38.677 --> 00:19:42.447
In the military it would be
like motorcycles on base,

00:19:42.447 --> 00:19:44.283
so they didn't want you to
ride a motorcycle on base,

00:19:44.283 --> 00:19:46.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
so first, they did, "Okay,
you have to take this course."

00:19:46.552 --> 00:19:47.986
Okay, so you have to
take like three days

00:19:47.986 --> 00:19:49.254 line:0%
to take this course,

00:19:49.254 --> 00:19:50.455 line:0%
so okay, you gotta
take this course.

00:19:50.455 --> 00:19:51.924 line:0%
You have to do like an up.

00:19:51.924 --> 00:19:53.692 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
You do it every year, and
then you have to have gloves.

00:19:53.692 --> 00:19:55.127
You have to have shoes
that cover your ankle.

00:19:55.127 --> 00:19:57.462 align:start size:97% position:3%
You have to have long sleeves,
helmet, reflector vest.

00:19:57.462 --> 00:19:59.698
They started adding all these
administrative requirements,

00:19:59.698 --> 00:20:01.133 align:start size:84% position:16%
and finally, it's
like, "Okay, you win.

00:20:01.133 --> 00:20:02.634
"I'm exhausted.

00:20:02.634 --> 00:20:04.036
"This whole year is gonna
be trying to figure out

00:20:04.036 --> 00:20:06.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
"how to ride my
motorcycle onto base here.

00:20:06.305 --> 00:20:07.706
"Fine, you've got it,"

00:20:07.706 --> 00:20:09.875
so that's kind of
what governments do

00:20:09.875 --> 00:20:11.043
to try to make it if they,

00:20:11.043 --> 00:20:12.678 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Oh, we're not
making guns illegal."

00:20:12.678 --> 00:20:15.214
Well, you're making
them administratively
impossible to get,

00:20:15.214 --> 00:20:16.882
like you've done with
concealed carry permits

00:20:16.882 --> 00:20:18.217
in a lot of states.

00:20:18.217 --> 00:20:19.651
- That's actually
a perfect segue

00:20:19.651 --> 00:20:22.054 align:start position:0%
to what I wanted to get to
next relative to just generally

00:20:22.054 --> 00:20:23.589 align:start size:81% position:19%
what's going on
with the military.

00:20:23.589 --> 00:20:27.492
What do you make of the state
of the military at the moment

00:20:27.492 --> 00:20:30.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the state of the
way that it seems

00:20:30.629 --> 00:20:33.298 align:start size:84% position:16%
politics has really
infected the military,

00:20:33.298 --> 00:20:36.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
at least in the last,
say, 5, 10 years,

00:20:36.335 --> 00:20:38.136 align:start size:84% position:16%
that it didn't
seem it did before,

00:20:38.136 --> 00:20:40.672
or maybe we didn't
know about it before?

00:20:40.672 --> 00:20:43.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yep, so obviously,
politics has been creeping

00:20:43.342 --> 00:20:46.445
into all aspects of our
culture for a long time.

00:20:46.445 --> 00:20:47.779 align:start size:84% position:16%
People like to
point to Hollywood,

00:20:47.779 --> 00:20:49.781 align:start size:84% position:16%
but hey, look at
there's Wall Street,

00:20:49.781 --> 00:20:51.083
more and more into sports.

00:20:51.083 --> 00:20:53.685
I mean, almost every,
education, of course,

00:20:53.685 --> 00:20:55.287
but really, every
aspect of society.

00:20:55.287 --> 00:20:57.289
We thought the military
was immune from that,

00:20:57.289 --> 00:20:59.758
and we found out, over
the last couple years,

00:20:59.758 --> 00:21:02.127
that it is most certainly not,

00:21:02.127 --> 00:21:03.562
and really, not even
talking about that,

00:21:03.562 --> 00:21:05.497 align:start size:97% position:3%
the wokeness in the military
or anything like that.

00:21:05.497 --> 00:21:09.434
We can just go and look at
20 years of our experience

00:21:09.434 --> 00:21:11.136
in Iraq and Afghanistan

00:21:11.136 --> 00:21:13.472 align:start size:88% position:13%
and how none of our
senior military leaders

00:21:13.472 --> 00:21:16.008
have been held accountable for
the decisions that they made,

00:21:16.008 --> 00:21:17.209
and we can go back

00:21:17.209 --> 00:21:18.510
and look at each and
every one of these guys

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:19.778
with stars on their shoulders

00:21:19.778 --> 00:21:21.013
sitting in front of Congress

00:21:21.013 --> 00:21:23.348
saying the exact same
things year after year.

00:21:23.348 --> 00:21:24.850
You can take the year off there,

00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:26.084
and it's a different person,

00:21:26.084 --> 00:21:28.920
but the audio will
be exactly the same:

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
"(indistinct) meeting
our milestones."

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:32.424
"The Afghan National Army

00:21:32.424 --> 00:21:35.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
"is getting closer to being
able to stand up on their own.

00:21:35.093 --> 00:21:37.095 align:start size:88% position:13%
"We just need more
resources, more people,

00:21:37.095 --> 00:21:40.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
"more funding, more
equipment," whatever it is,

00:21:40.299 --> 00:21:43.502
and then "The Afghanistan
Papers" was published,

00:21:43.502 --> 00:21:45.337
and I hope more people read
"The Afghanistan Papers"

00:21:45.337 --> 00:21:46.705
by Craig Whitlock

00:21:46.705 --> 00:21:48.874
because what he does is
takes these interviews

00:21:48.874 --> 00:21:50.175
that these senior-level leaders

00:21:50.175 --> 00:21:52.110
thought were gonna
remain classified,

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:53.712
juxtaposes it with
what they were saying

00:21:53.712 --> 00:21:55.781
in front of Congress, so
to the American people,

00:21:55.781 --> 00:21:57.249
to Congress, to their troops,

00:21:57.249 --> 00:21:59.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
and essentially, you
have two separate stories

00:21:59.718 --> 00:22:01.586
180 out from one another,

00:22:01.586 --> 00:22:03.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so they're essentially
lying to their troops,

00:22:03.922 --> 00:22:05.891
lying to Congress, lying
to the American people,

00:22:05.891 --> 00:22:08.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
and these senior-level
leaders continue to fail up,

00:22:08.994 --> 00:22:10.295
and we see what happens

00:22:10.295 --> 00:22:12.431
when we continue to do
that year after year

00:22:12.431 --> 00:22:14.399 align:start size:88% position:13%
and hold no senior-level
leaders accountable.

00:22:14.399 --> 00:22:16.501 align:start size:84% position:16%
We have a withdrawal
from Afghanistan

00:22:16.501 --> 00:22:18.503
that looks like what
we saw last August,

00:22:18.503 --> 00:22:20.872 align:start position:0%
which the media, of course,
doesn't even talk about anymore,

00:22:20.872 --> 00:22:23.175 align:start size:88% position:13%
but we all got to watch
with our own eyes,

00:22:23.175 --> 00:22:25.310 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it's also why
everyone in this country

00:22:25.310 --> 00:22:27.245
with a little bit
of common sense

00:22:27.245 --> 00:22:28.847
could comment on it

00:22:28.847 --> 00:22:30.615
because you didn't need to have

00:22:30.615 --> 00:22:32.217
a touchpoint with the military

00:22:32.217 --> 00:22:35.454
or study strategy or tactics
or military history at all.

00:22:35.454 --> 00:22:38.023
You could just look at that
and apply some common sense,

00:22:38.023 --> 00:22:39.925
which is something that
Carl von Clausewitz said

00:22:39.925 --> 00:22:42.260
was most important aspect
of any military leader,

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:44.363
any combat leader is
having common sense,

00:22:44.363 --> 00:22:45.697
and so anyone could look at that

00:22:45.697 --> 00:22:47.165
and say, "Why are we giving up

00:22:47.165 --> 00:22:49.868 align:start size:97% position:3%
"this tactically advantageous
position at Bagram

00:22:49.868 --> 00:22:51.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
"and putting America's
sons and daughters

00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:53.672 align:start size:88% position:13%
"into tactically
disadvantageous position

00:22:53.672 --> 00:22:57.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
"when we've had 20 years to
prepare for this eventuality?"

00:22:57.976 --> 00:23:00.912 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this is the best the
United States military can do?

00:23:00.912 --> 00:23:02.914
This is the best that we have.

00:23:02.914 --> 00:23:06.184
This is what are giving
our sons and daughters

00:23:06.184 --> 00:23:07.719 align:start size:84% position:16%
as we're putting them
in this position.

00:23:07.719 --> 00:23:09.221
Now, if you'd done that
at the tactical level,

00:23:09.221 --> 00:23:11.523
as an O-3, so as a
captain in the Army

00:23:11.523 --> 00:23:13.458 align:start size:97% position:3%
or a lieutenant in the
Navy or something like that

00:23:13.458 --> 00:23:16.094
or as a chief in the Navy
or something, a sergeant,

00:23:16.094 --> 00:23:18.430
you'd be court-martialed,
sent home if you just...

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
And our senior-level
leaders, what do they do?

00:23:20.599 --> 00:23:22.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
All those people are
still in those positions.

00:23:22.434 --> 00:23:23.902
If they've retired,

00:23:23.902 --> 00:23:25.904 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're on the board at
Raytheon or General Dynamics

00:23:25.904 --> 00:23:27.305
or something like that,

00:23:27.305 --> 00:23:29.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
so when we look at
lobbyists and politicians

00:23:29.374 --> 00:23:32.978 align:start size:97% position:3%
and think tanks and that
military-industrial complex,

00:23:32.978 --> 00:23:33.812
that's a real thing.

00:23:33.812 --> 00:23:34.746
That's an animal,

00:23:34.746 --> 00:23:37.449
and when it's not held to check

00:23:37.449 --> 00:23:38.450
and no one's held accountable,

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:39.851
you get things

00:23:39.851 --> 00:23:41.353 align:start size:97% position:3%
like the disastrous
withdrawal from Afghanistan,

00:23:41.353 --> 00:23:43.188
essentially rushing to our death

00:23:43.188 --> 00:23:46.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
after 20 years of
being able to prepare

00:23:46.258 --> 00:23:47.926
and plan for that eventuality.

00:23:47.926 --> 00:23:49.294
It's inexcusable.

00:23:49.294 --> 00:23:50.762
- So I don't know
if this about me,

00:23:50.762 --> 00:23:53.432
but every August, I go
off the grid for a month:

00:23:53.432 --> 00:23:57.302
no phone, no news,
no communication with
the outside world.

00:23:57.302 --> 00:23:58.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
I literally disappear
for a month,

00:23:58.837 --> 00:24:01.606 align:start size:88% position:13%
so the August, this
past August, obviously,

00:24:01.606 --> 00:24:03.108
was the Afghanistan withdrawal,

00:24:03.108 --> 00:24:05.310
so I did not know that
until September 1st.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:07.412
I come back and I
have a guest host.

00:24:07.412 --> 00:24:09.181 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was Adam Carolla
this past year

00:24:09.181 --> 00:24:11.183
who kinda caught me up
to speed on everything,

00:24:11.183 --> 00:24:13.952
so I was seeing all those
videos two weeks later

00:24:13.952 --> 00:24:16.721
all in real time for the first
time in front of my audience,

00:24:16.721 --> 00:24:19.991 align:start size:97% position:3%
and what I said to Adam
watching them in real time was

00:24:19.991 --> 00:24:21.193
from what I could tell,

00:24:21.193 --> 00:24:22.360
just seeing it all
right in front of me,

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:24.362 align:start size:84% position:16%
right at that
moment, all at once,

00:24:24.362 --> 00:24:26.498
that this could not
have been worse,

00:24:26.498 --> 00:24:29.568 align:start size:97% position:3%
that if you had intentionally
tried to bungle something

00:24:29.568 --> 00:24:31.036
at the highest level,

00:24:31.036 --> 00:24:33.405 align:start size:97% position:3%
that that's pretty much what
it would've looked like.

00:24:33.405 --> 00:24:34.906
Do you think that's
a fair estimation?

00:24:34.906 --> 00:24:37.209 align:start position:0%
I mean, 20 years from now, when
we look back on this thing,

00:24:37.209 --> 00:24:39.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
what do you think the
postmortem is gonna look like?

00:24:40.912 --> 00:24:42.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, if it's
honest, it's gonna be

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:44.182
exactly what you what you
just said right there.

00:24:44.182 --> 00:24:46.485
You could not have
bungled this worse

00:24:46.485 --> 00:24:48.453
had you actually been trying,

00:24:48.453 --> 00:24:50.589 align:start size:94% position:6%
and once again, it's that
accountability piece,

00:24:50.589 --> 00:24:52.157
but what we had,

00:24:52.157 --> 00:24:56.695
we had 20 years of our own
experience to look back on,

00:24:56.695 --> 00:24:59.531 align:start size:97% position:3%
to take lessons from, to
apply going forward as wisdom.

00:24:59.531 --> 00:25:01.032
We didn't even have to
go back to the Soviets.

00:25:01.032 --> 00:25:03.802 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, we should have earlier
on gone back to '79 to '89,

00:25:03.802 --> 00:25:05.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Soviet experience
in Afghanistan.

00:25:05.904 --> 00:25:08.139
We didn't have to go back to
the three British incursions

00:25:08.139 --> 00:25:09.908
in the 1800s, early 1900s.

00:25:09.908 --> 00:25:11.109
Certainly didn't have to go back

00:25:11.109 --> 00:25:12.511 align:start size:84% position:16%
to Alexander the
Great, Genghis Khan,

00:25:12.511 --> 00:25:13.912
although we could have,

00:25:13.912 --> 00:25:15.380 align:start position:0%
and we could have taken lessons
from all of those things

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:18.416 align:start size:97% position:3%
and applied them to our
present situation as wisdom,

00:25:18.416 --> 00:25:19.918
yet we did not.

00:25:19.918 --> 00:25:21.486
We think of things in terms
of four-year election cycles,

00:25:21.486 --> 00:25:22.754
eight-year election cycles

00:25:22.754 --> 00:25:24.756
for the real deep
thinkers among us,

00:25:24.756 --> 00:25:26.358
but what we neglect to do is

00:25:26.358 --> 00:25:28.727 align:start size:97% position:3%
go back in the pages of
history, take those lessons,

00:25:28.727 --> 00:25:30.962
and apply them going forward,

00:25:30.962 --> 00:25:32.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's obviously
to the detriment

00:25:32.697 --> 00:25:34.499
of our sons and daughters

00:25:34.499 --> 00:25:35.934
who are out there
on the front line,

00:25:35.934 --> 00:25:38.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we saw in Afghanistan,
13 lost their lives,

00:25:38.203 --> 00:25:40.171
to say nothing of those
who lost arms and legs

00:25:40.171 --> 00:25:41.406
and can't walk anymore

00:25:41.406 --> 00:25:43.308 align:start size:88% position:13%
and are dealing with the
post-traumatic stress

00:25:43.308 --> 00:25:44.509
or traumatic brain injury

00:25:44.509 --> 00:25:46.678 align:start size:84% position:16%
from a position that
they were put in

00:25:46.678 --> 00:25:47.913
by our senior-level leaders.

00:25:47.913 --> 00:25:49.381
- Is it crazy to you

00:25:49.381 --> 00:25:51.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
how quickly we just move
off things like that?

00:25:51.483 --> 00:25:52.717
I mean, as you said,

00:25:52.717 --> 00:25:53.952
it was the longest war
in American history.

00:25:53.952 --> 00:25:55.186
We were there for 20 years.

00:25:55.186 --> 00:25:56.688
It was very unclear
to a lot of people

00:25:56.688 --> 00:25:59.024
sort of why were there and
how it was connected to 9/11

00:25:59.024 --> 00:26:00.392
and all of that stuff,

00:26:00.392 --> 00:26:03.528
but that basically we
had two or three weeks

00:26:03.528 --> 00:26:05.764 align:start size:81% position:19%
of sort of news
cycle and outrage,

00:26:05.764 --> 00:26:08.466 align:start size:88% position:13%
and now it's completely
not talked about,

00:26:08.466 --> 00:26:11.269 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we've got this situation
in Ukraine right now,

00:26:11.269 --> 00:26:13.204 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's like do
our allies trust us?

00:26:13.204 --> 00:26:14.973
Does anyone believe
what we're doing?

00:26:14.973 --> 00:26:16.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
Do we have the right
people in charge?

00:26:16.808 --> 00:26:17.776
Any of that.

00:26:18.944 --> 00:26:21.613
- Yeah, no, those are
valid, valid questions,

00:26:21.613 --> 00:26:23.348
and as it pertains
to what's going on

00:26:23.348 --> 00:26:24.583
in Afghanistan vis-a-vis,

00:26:24.583 --> 00:26:26.384
or in Ukraine vis-a-vis Russia,

00:26:26.384 --> 00:26:28.987
once again, we had a lot of data

00:26:28.987 --> 00:26:30.989 align:start size:97% position:3%
which to look at and predict
what was gonna happen.

00:26:30.989 --> 00:26:32.457
In my second novel,
"True Believer,"

00:26:32.457 --> 00:26:35.393 align:start position:0%
I actually use a false flag
operation that came out in 2019,

00:26:35.393 --> 00:26:37.228
but I use a false flag operation

00:26:37.228 --> 00:26:41.366
to instigate a Russian
invasion of Ukraine,

00:26:41.366 --> 00:26:45.070 align:start position:0%
and I got that just by studying
the situation at the time,

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:47.238
so I was writing
that in 2016, 2017,

00:26:50.208 --> 00:26:52.877
editing it in 2018 so it
could come out in 2019,

00:26:52.877 --> 00:26:55.747
but that research should
have shown anybody that,

00:26:55.747 --> 00:26:57.515 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Hey, this is
essentially inevitable"

00:26:57.515 --> 00:27:01.820 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the Russian population
is in a steep decline

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:04.522
and has been since the
end of the Cold War,

00:27:04.522 --> 00:27:07.058
and they are very, a
couple generations away

00:27:07.058 --> 00:27:08.727
from there being
no ethnic Russians,

00:27:08.727 --> 00:27:09.961
and they're certainly very close

00:27:09.961 --> 00:27:11.763
to not being able
to field an army

00:27:11.763 --> 00:27:15.066 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the numbers that they have
been able to in the past,

00:27:15.066 --> 00:27:18.303
and at the time,
it was about 2022.

00:27:18.303 --> 00:27:20.739 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's actually a book called
"The Accidental Superpower"

00:27:20.739 --> 00:27:23.875
while Peter Zeihan looks at
geography and demographics

00:27:23.875 --> 00:27:25.610 align:start size:88% position:13%
as far as world
history and superpowers

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:27.379
and how that has has played in,

00:27:27.379 --> 00:27:32.350 align:start size:97% position:3%
and yeah, 2022 was about the
last year that Russia had

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:34.386 align:start size:88% position:13%
before they would not
be able to field an army

00:27:34.386 --> 00:27:36.021
capable of invading Ukraine

00:27:36.021 --> 00:27:38.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they need
those ethnic Russians,

00:27:38.356 --> 00:27:39.991
and Ukraine has the
largest population

00:27:39.991 --> 00:27:43.028
of ethnic Russians in the
world outside of Russia,

00:27:43.028 --> 00:27:44.329
so these things
were predictable,

00:27:44.329 --> 00:27:45.530
and then, of course,

00:27:45.530 --> 00:27:46.731
we can go back to World
War I, World War II,

00:27:46.731 --> 00:27:48.600 align:start size:84% position:16%
end of the Cold
War, promises made.

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:50.902
We rarely put ourselves
in our enemy's shoes,

00:27:50.902 --> 00:27:54.205
and red game things
from that position,

00:27:54.205 --> 00:27:55.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
red cell things
from that position,

00:27:55.607 --> 00:27:57.609
but we should do that more often

00:27:57.609 --> 00:27:59.444
because we'd make
wiser decisions.

00:27:59.444 --> 00:28:00.945
- What do you think

00:28:00.945 --> 00:28:03.615 align:start size:88% position:13%
the role of the
American military should
be at this point?

00:28:03.615 --> 00:28:06.451 align:start size:88% position:13%
It seems like it really
has sorta changed,

00:28:06.451 --> 00:28:09.287 align:start position:0%
or it seemed like it was
changing, at least, under Trump.

00:28:09.287 --> 00:28:11.756 align:start size:84% position:16%
There basically, there
was a lot of peace.

00:28:11.756 --> 00:28:13.892 align:start size:97% position:3%
There was a lot of like, "Oh,
if we mess with this guy,

00:28:13.892 --> 00:28:15.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
"we don't know what
he's gonna do."

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:18.430
You remember he dropped that
mother of all bombs in Iraq.

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:19.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
Nobody understood
why, and it was like,

00:28:19.864 --> 00:28:21.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Well, it got pretty
quiet there pretty quick,"

00:28:21.599 --> 00:28:25.670
and now we're in this
Ukraine situation.

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.772
What do you think
the general role

00:28:27.772 --> 00:28:30.275
of America worldwide should be?

00:28:31.409 --> 00:28:34.779
- Well, it should be
one of respect and fear

00:28:34.779 --> 00:28:36.981
that goes along with that

00:28:36.981 --> 00:28:38.683 align:start size:84% position:16%
because that military
is really what,

00:28:38.683 --> 00:28:41.186 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's like the Second Amendment
to the First Amendment.

00:28:41.186 --> 00:28:43.354
The military, it
backs up everything

00:28:43.354 --> 00:28:44.989
that we say and that we do,

00:28:44.989 --> 00:28:48.093 align:start size:81% position:19%
so if we have this
strong military

00:28:48.093 --> 00:28:50.195 align:start size:97% position:3%
that is more concerned
with other people's borders

00:28:50.195 --> 00:28:52.063
rather than our own,

00:28:52.063 --> 00:28:53.965 align:start size:88% position:13%
while essentially,
we're seeing an invasion

00:28:53.965 --> 00:28:55.400
across our southern
border right now

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:56.668
that's, once again,

00:28:56.668 --> 00:28:59.604
not really talked about
in the mainstream press.

00:28:59.604 --> 00:29:01.539
It's been going on
for quite some time,

00:29:01.539 --> 00:29:03.775
but we have all sorts
of issues like that,

00:29:03.775 --> 00:29:06.745
and we have a Department of
Defense after World War II.

00:29:06.745 --> 00:29:09.114
It used to be the Department
of War, the War Department.

00:29:09.114 --> 00:29:11.116
Then it changed, 1947,

00:29:11.116 --> 00:29:12.350
and really, since then,

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:14.652
we haven't done very
well in conflicts,

00:29:14.652 --> 00:29:18.056
and that's the exact same time
that accountability shifted,

00:29:18.056 --> 00:29:19.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
and by shifted, I
mean that we started

00:29:19.891 --> 00:29:22.060 align:start size:97% position:3%
not holding our senior-level
leaders accountable.

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:23.561
When we talk about
George Marshall,

00:29:23.561 --> 00:29:25.964 align:start position:0%
he's most known for the Marshall
Plan after World War II,

00:29:25.964 --> 00:29:29.501
but really, what's more
important is what he did

00:29:29.501 --> 00:29:32.003
in their lead-up to World War
II and during World War II,

00:29:32.003 --> 00:29:34.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
which were fire those
leaders who didn't cut it,

00:29:34.506 --> 00:29:36.407
so he put all those
guys in positions

00:29:36.407 --> 00:29:39.410
whose names we know today
as leading us through,

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:42.347 align:start size:88% position:13%
that led us through
World War II to victory,

00:29:42.347 --> 00:29:43.848
but had he not done that

00:29:43.848 --> 00:29:45.784
and just let those people
who were in those places

00:29:45.784 --> 00:29:47.318
that weren't cutting it,

00:29:47.318 --> 00:29:48.753
I mean, who knows how World
War II would've turned out,

00:29:48.753 --> 00:29:52.390 align:start position:0%
but he fired people that didn't
cut it after World War II,

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:54.292
and we shift over to the
Department of Defense,

00:29:54.292 --> 00:29:56.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
things start to
change: Korea, Vietnam,

00:29:56.594 --> 00:29:58.329 align:start size:81% position:19%
and obviously,
what we see today,

00:29:58.329 --> 00:30:00.565
and we, for whatever
reason, we're hesitant

00:30:00.565 --> 00:30:03.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
to hold these senior-level
leaders accountable,

00:30:03.201 --> 00:30:04.569
but we can trace
it all the way back

00:30:04.569 --> 00:30:07.438
to the end of World
War II and in 1947,

00:30:07.438 --> 00:30:11.709
but today, we're in an
interesting position

00:30:11.709 --> 00:30:13.845
in that, yes, we
have a technologic,

00:30:13.845 --> 00:30:15.713
we have 20 years of experience

00:30:15.713 --> 00:30:17.315
and we have this technology

00:30:17.315 --> 00:30:19.017 align:start size:81% position:19%
that has just been
racing forward

00:30:19.017 --> 00:30:21.886
when it comes to
our weapon systems,

00:30:21.886 --> 00:30:24.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so we take those
tactical lessons learned,

00:30:24.255 --> 00:30:26.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
take these new weapons
and the technology

00:30:26.825 --> 00:30:29.260 align:start position:0%
as far as tracking and targeting
and all the rest of it.

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:30.495
We should have

00:30:30.495 --> 00:30:34.265 align:start size:88% position:13%
the most feared
military on the planet,

00:30:34.265 --> 00:30:37.969
yet we have a border that's
just porous, essentially,

00:30:37.969 --> 00:30:40.138
and we see what we
saw in Afghanistan,

00:30:40.138 --> 00:30:42.807 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
so with all that technology,
all those lessons,

00:30:42.807 --> 00:30:45.076 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
we still get that
outcome in Afghanistan,

00:30:45.076 --> 00:30:46.778 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
so if I'm the enemy,
and I'm looking at that,

00:30:46.778 --> 00:30:48.046 line:0%
that gives me some wiggle room,

00:30:48.046 --> 00:30:49.247 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that gives me
some lessons learned

00:30:49.247 --> 00:30:50.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
to apply to my
future battle plans

00:30:50.882 --> 00:30:53.985 align:start position:0%
that probably go more than
four to eight years in advance.

00:30:53.985 --> 00:30:55.153
I can think decades.

00:30:55.153 --> 00:30:56.588 align:start size:88% position:13%
I can think even
centuries if I'm China,

00:30:56.588 --> 00:31:00.124 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I can apply these lessons
to those battle plans.

00:31:00.124 --> 00:31:03.027
- So it's not that
hopeful, I sense,

00:31:03.027 --> 00:31:04.329 align:start size:88% position:13%
is-
- Try to remain hopeful.

00:31:04.329 --> 00:31:06.097 align:start size:72% position:28%
Try. Try.
(Dave laughs)

00:31:06.097 --> 00:31:07.632 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, my audience
knows I always try to,

00:31:07.632 --> 00:31:09.200
I try to talk about
the serious stuff,

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:10.735
and then I try to,

00:31:10.735 --> 00:31:12.237
there's gotta be something
on the horizon, some hope.

00:31:12.237 --> 00:31:14.305
I mean, what are some of
the mechanisms we could do

00:31:14.305 --> 00:31:15.173
to fix some of this stuff?

00:31:15.173 --> 00:31:16.274
I mean, I don't sense

00:31:16.274 --> 00:31:17.809 align:start size:88% position:13%
that this administration
is gonna go in

00:31:17.809 --> 00:31:19.043
and fix the things
you're talking about

00:31:19.043 --> 00:31:20.812
'cause they seem to be
aiding and abetting it.

00:31:20.812 --> 00:31:21.880
You talk about the
southern border.

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:23.081
It's like they're the ones

00:31:23.081 --> 00:31:24.616 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are freaking
opening this thing up.

00:31:24.616 --> 00:31:28.486
900,000 new people roughly in
the country in the last year,

00:31:28.486 --> 00:31:30.955 align:start position:0%
according to Mayorkas from
Department of Homeland Security,

00:31:30.955 --> 00:31:35.326 align:start size:97% position:3%
so what are some of the things
that maybe could be done

00:31:35.326 --> 00:31:37.528
to reverse some of
the damage here?

00:31:37.528 --> 00:31:39.397 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, first, it's like
the top three things:

00:31:39.397 --> 00:31:40.999
holding senior-level
leaders accountable,

00:31:40.999 --> 00:31:43.401
just like you hold
junior-level leaders

00:31:43.401 --> 00:31:47.338 align:start size:97% position:3%
or Soldiers, Sailors, Airmen,
and Marines accountable,

00:31:47.338 --> 00:31:48.206
so that's one.

00:31:48.206 --> 00:31:49.674
That sends a message

00:31:49.674 --> 00:31:51.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause when you don't,
what message does that send

00:31:51.209 --> 00:31:52.877 align:start size:97% position:3%
to those people who wanna
put a star on their shoulder

00:31:52.877 --> 00:31:54.913
or wanna put another
one on their shoulder?

00:31:54.913 --> 00:31:56.481
You're sending a message,

00:31:56.481 --> 00:31:57.849
regardless of which one of
those things that you do,

00:31:57.849 --> 00:31:59.684
so holding those
guys accountable,

00:31:59.684 --> 00:32:00.919
one of the top priorities.

00:32:00.919 --> 00:32:03.087 align:start size:84% position:16%
Next one, border,
sealing that border.

00:32:03.087 --> 00:32:04.555
That has to happen,

00:32:04.555 --> 00:32:06.424
and then, third biggest one

00:32:06.424 --> 00:32:08.526
is becoming less
reliant on China,

00:32:08.526 --> 00:32:11.596
particularly where it comes to
technology side of the house

00:32:11.596 --> 00:32:13.531
and pharmaceutical
side of the house,

00:32:13.531 --> 00:32:15.733
so things that we're, I
mean, it's just insane

00:32:15.733 --> 00:32:18.703
that we are reliant on
essentially our enemy

00:32:18.703 --> 00:32:22.006
for things that are paramount

00:32:22.006 --> 00:32:23.808
to our national security,

00:32:23.808 --> 00:32:27.879 align:start position:0%
so those three things right
there, and they seem so simple.

00:32:27.879 --> 00:32:30.181
Once again, apply common
sense to this problem.

00:32:30.181 --> 00:32:31.616
I mean, that's what I
did on the battlefield.

00:32:31.616 --> 00:32:33.117
You just look at the
chaos that's going on.

00:32:33.117 --> 00:32:35.053
You look for gaps in
the enemy's defenses.

00:32:35.053 --> 00:32:36.621 align:start size:84% position:16%
You look to capitalize
on momentum.

00:32:36.621 --> 00:32:38.056
You look to adapt

00:32:38.056 --> 00:32:39.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the enemy's
certainly adapting to you.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:40.992 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's what I do in
the pages of my novels,

00:32:40.992 --> 00:32:42.393
and you can do that same thing

00:32:42.393 --> 00:32:44.095 align:start size:84% position:16%
looking strategically
at an issue,

00:32:44.095 --> 00:32:46.064 align:start size:84% position:16%
so those three
things, I think, are,

00:32:47.198 --> 00:32:51.302 align:start size:88% position:13%
as simple as they sound,
somehow escape the,

00:32:51.302 --> 00:32:53.071
I don't know, the thought
process and the strategies

00:32:53.071 --> 00:32:55.940 align:start position:0%
being put forth by our
senior-level elected officials,

